# Border

Use this guidance when choosing borders, dividers, selected indicators, or keyboard focus rings for
Atlassian UI. Start with the existing ADS component treatment. For custom UI, choose the border's
purpose before its width and color.

Borders communicate boundaries and state. They do not supply interaction behavior, accessible names,
selection semantics, or elevation.

## Choose the treatment from its purpose

| Purpose                                      | Width                   | Color                   | Application                                                                    |
| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Flat grouping or divider                     | `border.width`          | `color.border`          | Separate content when whitespace or a surface alone is insufficient.           |
| Neutral boundary requiring stronger contrast | `border.width`          | `color.border.bold`     | Use when the boundary must be perceivable; check the actual adjacent surfaces. |
| Form control boundary                        | `border.width`          | `color.border.input`    | Prefer the form component's supplied treatment.                                |
| Persistent selected indicator                | `border.width.selected` | `color.border.selected` | Identify a selected or opened item, such as a tab or list option.              |
| Keyboard focus ring                          | `border.width.focused`  | `color.border.focused`  | Use the component's focus treatment or Focusable for custom interactive UI.    |
| Invalid form control                         | `border.width`          | `color.border.danger`   | Pair the boundary with the control's error message and invalid semantics.      |
| Disabled element with a border               | `border.width`          | `color.border.disabled` | Follow the component's disabled appearance and behavior.                       |

Token values can change. Use token references rather than copying their current pixel, rem, or color
values. Border width, color, style, and radius are separate decisions. Set an appropriate border
style for a custom boundary; width and color alone do not make a CSS border visible.

The table describes custom treatments, not overrides for ADS components. Preserve an existing valid
component appearance, including components that use an underline or another indicator instead of an
enclosing border.

## Preserve component-owned treatments

Reuse ADS controls before recreating their states. Read the selected component's guidance before
customizing its border, selected indicator, or focus ring. Avoid wrapping an ADS control in another
visible focus ring when it already supplies one.

For custom interactive elements that need a focus treatment, use the supported Focusable pattern and
retain the element's keyboard behavior and semantics. Focusable supplies focus styling and renders a
button by default; a custom host element still needs its appropriate semantics and keyboard
behavior. It does not implement selection. Retrieve its API before composing it with the host
element.

For Figma work, use the ADS Focus Ring component. Code and design should preserve the same focus
meaning without requiring identical implementation mechanics.

## Keep selection and keyboard focus distinct

Pair `border.width.selected` with `color.border.selected` for a custom selected indicator. Pair
`border.width.focused` with `color.border.focused` for a custom focus ring.

Selection can persist after focus moves away. A selected item can also receive keyboard focus: keep
its selected indicator and show the focus ring independently. Do not replace selection with focus
styling, or use a thicker selected border as the only keyboard focus cue.

An invalid field still needs a visible focus indicator when focused. Preserve the invalid boundary,
error message, and focus treatment together rather than choosing one state to hide the other. Hover
and press should retain relevant selected, invalid, and focus cues. Use the component's state
behavior; do not invent tokens by appending `.hovered` or `.pressed` to a border token.

Avoid layout shifts as indicators appear or change width. Follow the component's existing
implementation. For custom UI, reserve the necessary space or use a supported indicator that does
not change layout; check text wrapping and target dimensions in each state.

## Choose semantic and accent colors deliberately

Use the color token whose documented purpose matches the boundary. Brand, status, and accent colors
are not interchangeable simply because they look similar in one theme.

- Status boundaries use the relevant semantic border family, such as `color.border.warning`,
  `color.border.success`, or `color.border.information`. Communicate necessary status with text or
  another recognizable cue as well as color.
- On bold backgrounds, consult `color.border.inverse` and the host component's guidance.
- For non-status categories or accent surfaces, retrieve the color accents guidance and the
  corresponding accent border token. A subtle accent background may need an accent border when the
  boundary communicates required information.
- Subtle status border variants are intended for decoration and may not meet the contrast needed for
  a required boundary. Do not substitute them solely to make a meaningful border less prominent.

`color.border` is useful for grouping and separation; it is not a guarantee of sufficient contrast
for every required control boundary. Token descriptions guide the choice, while the rendered
foreground/background pairing determines the result.

## Check the rendered boundary and focus ring

Where a boundary or state indicator conveys information needed to identify or operate the UI, check
the applicable 3:1 non-text contrast requirement against adjacent colors. Decorative dividers have a
different role; do not turn every separator into a bold border to satisfy a blanket rule.

Check supported themes and relevant states on their actual host surfaces. Resolve translucent
backgrounds over the surface beneath them before calculating contrast. A light-theme screenshot or a
token name alone does not establish dark-theme accessibility.

Exercise keyboard focus through the real interaction path. Confirm the ring is visible, is not
clipped by overflow, and remains distinguishable on selected or invalid elements. Preserve a usable
focus indicator when customizing styles; do not remove the outline without a working replacement.

Use whitespace, surface treatment, or a border according to the intended hierarchy. Borders can
separate sections within an overlay without adding another shadow. For floating surfaces or scroll
boundaries, consult elevation guidance rather than treating a border as a complete layering recipe.

## Retrieve only the missing detail

When loaded through the ADS skill, follow its retrieval order.

Use the border token reference for widths, the color token reference for border colors, and
component references for APIs and behavior. Border radius belongs to the shape guidance; do not
infer a radius from the border width.

Use the guidance here first. If a detail is missing, use `atlas ads`; use ADS MCP only after a CLI
error, an empty result, or a missing capability. For tokens, search by purpose, then confirm the
exact token:

```sh
atlas ads search "selected border" --type token --limit 5 --json
atlas ads token border.width.selected --json
atlas ads token color.border.selected --json
```

For MCP fallback, use `ads_search_tokens` for token usage, `ads_search_components` for component
APIs, or `ads_get_a11y_guidelines` for accessibility; follow each tool’s exposed schema. Don’t
repeat a successful lookup through another route, fetch the full catalog, or construct tokens by
appending state suffixes. If a detail remains unresolved, label it unverified and state what still
needs checking.

## Verify the result

Check the cases relevant to the task:

- The boundary's purpose justifies its width and color.
- Selected and focused indicators use their documented token pairings.
- Focus remains visible on selected and invalid elements, including during hover or press.
- State changes do not move surrounding content or clip the focus indicator.
- Meaningful boundaries and state cues remain perceivable on the actual surfaces in supported
  themes.
- Existing ADS controls retain their built-in behavior and do not acquire duplicate focus rings.

Report token verification separately from rendered and keyboard checks. State which themes and
states were checked, and identify unverified behavior without claiming it passed.

## Sources

Design principles: [ADS border](https://atlassian.design/foundations/border). Exact token names and
purposes: the ADS border and color token catalogs. Component guidance supplies APIs and interaction
behavior; color and color accents guidance supplies semantic pairing and contrast context.

# Color

Use color tokens to communicate intent, hierarchy, and interaction in Atlassian UI. Start with the
existing ADS component treatment; use these choices when styling custom UI or reviewing a token
choice.

## Choose the token family

1. Identify what the color communicates. Use a semantic role for status, validation, actions, or
   discovery. Use an accent when the hue identifies a category and could change without changing its
   meaning. See the Color accents guidance for hue and pairing choices.
2. Choose the background emphasis that fits the intended hierarchy.
3. Pair it with the text, icon, and border tokens for that role and emphasis.
4. For interactive UI, apply the state to the element that owns the interaction. Preserve separate
   cues for persistent selection and keyboard focus.

Apply tokens with `token('color...')` from `@atlaskit/tokens`. Tokens resolve for light and dark
themes, so use their semantic names instead of copying palette values or hardcoded colors.

## Read token names

Most tokens follow `color.[property].[role].[emphasis].[state]`:

- **Property** describes where the color is applied: `background`, `text`, `icon`, or `border`.
- **Role** describes its intent: `brand`, `danger`, `warning`, `success`, `information`,
  `discovery`, `neutral`, `selected`, `focused`, `input`, or `accent.[hue]`.
- **Emphasis** describes contrast against the default surface: `bold`, `bolder`, `boldest`,
  `subtle`, `subtler`, or `subtlest`.
- **State** describes an interaction state such as `hovered` or `pressed`.

Accent tokens place the hue after the role, for example `color.background.accent.blue.subtlest`.
Look up exceptions such as `color.link`, `color.blanket`, `color.skeleton`, and `color.chart.*` in
the token catalog rather than extending the pattern.

## Choose a semantic role

| Role           | Choose it for                                     | Example use                         |
| :------------- | :------------------------------------------------ | :---------------------------------- |
| `danger`       | Destructive actions, errors, or critical failures | Error message or destructive action |
| `warning`      | Caution, risk, or something that needs attention  | Risk notice                         |
| `success`      | A successful, completed, or valid state           | Completion message                  |
| `information`  | A passive informational message                   | Informational notice                |
| `brand`        | Primary actions or product identity               | Primary action                      |
| `discovery`    | Discovery, AI, or a new feature                   | New feature callout                 |
| `neutral`      | Base surfaces and neutral layout                  | Page or control surface             |
| `input`        | Form field backgrounds and boundaries             | Text field                          |
| `selected`     | A persistent current choice                       | Selected row or option              |
| `focused`      | Keyboard focus indicator; border only             | Focus ring                          |
| `accent.[hue]` | Categories and labels whose hue is optional       | User-chosen tag                     |

Keep fixed meaning in the semantic role. For example, an error uses `danger` and a successful state
uses `success`; an accent red or green is for a category whose color does not carry that meaning.

## Pair semantic backgrounds and foregrounds

| Background                                              | Text and icon                                                 |
| :------------------------------------------------------ | :------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Semantic `*.bold` or `*.bolder`                         | `color.text.inverse` and `color.icon.inverse`                 |
| `color.background.warning.bold`                         | `color.text.warning.inverse` and `color.icon.warning.inverse` |
| Subtle, subtler, subtlest, or default semantic surfaces | Matching role text and icon, such as `color.text.danger`      |

Use the warning-specific inverse pair shown above on a bold warning background. Accent foreground
pairings are in the Color accents guidance.

```tsx
// Error banner
backgroundColor: token('color.background.danger.bold'),
color: token('color.text.inverse'),

// Category label whose hue carries no status meaning
backgroundColor: token('color.background.accent.red.bolder'),
color: token('color.text.inverse'),
```

## Apply state to the interactive element

### Form fields

Use `color.background.input` for the field background, with `color.background.input.hovered` and
`color.background.input.pressed` for those states. Use `color.border.input` for the field boundary.
Start with the ADS form component’s treatment when one owns the control.

### Selection and focus

Use `color.background.selected`, `color.text.selected`, `color.icon.selected`, or
`color.border.selected` for a persistent choice. Use `color.border.focused` for keyboard focus. When
an element is both selected and focused, keep the selected treatment and add the focus indicator.
Don’t use link, brand, or focused tokens to communicate selection. Pair selection color with another
cue, such as a checkmark, underline, border, or shape change, so selection remains clear without
color alone.

Apply `hovered` and `pressed` variants to the currently interactive element. Keep a selected item
recognizable during those temporary states. Icons do not have dedicated hovered or pressed color
tokens; use a subtle neutral background such as `color.background.neutral.subtle.hovered` or
`color.background.neutral.subtle.pressed` when the icon’s control needs a surface response.

## Check the rendered result

Inspect the foreground, meaningful borders, and state changes against the final visible background
in the themes the experience supports. For translucent colors, include the underlying surface when
checking contrast. Use `color.chart.*` for chart marks and product UI tokens for axes, labels, and
gridlines.

## Look up exact tokens

Search the ADS token registry when an identifier or pairing is uncertain:

```bash
atlas ads search "danger background" --type token
atlas ads token color.background.danger.bold --json
```

If the Atlas plugin is unavailable, use
`npx @atlaskit/ads-cli search "danger background" --type token`.

# Color accents

Use accents for categories, projects, and labels when their hue can change without changing their
meaning. Choose a semantic color role for status, alerts, validation, primary actions, and discovery
instead; see the Color guidance.

Start with the existing ADS component treatment for tags and labels. For custom UI, keep the
background, text, and icon in the same accent hue family.

**Available hues:** `blue`, `teal`, `green`, `lime`, `yellow`, `orange`, `red`, `magenta`, `purple`,
and `gray`.

Prefer another accent hue, such as orange, to yellow. Yellow accents can appear brown as their
values adjust to meet contrast requirements.

## Pair hue and emphasis

| Background emphasis     | Text and icon                                 |
| :---------------------- | :-------------------------------------------- |
| `subtlest` or `subtler` | Matching accent `default` or `bolder`         |
| `subtle`                | Matching accent `bolder`                      |
| `bolder`                | `color.text.inverse` and `color.icon.inverse` |

```tsx
// A category tag: the blue hue identifies a category, not a status
backgroundColor: token('color.background.accent.blue.subtlest'),
color: token('color.text.accent.blue'),
```

Use `color.chart.*` for chart marks. For other pairings, check the rendered foreground and
background: text needs 4.5:1 contrast and meaningful UI boundaries need 3:1.

## Choose borders by their purpose

Use `color.border.accent.[hue]` for a meaningful accent border on a non-bold accent background when
the boundary needs to meet 3:1 contrast. Verify the rendered pair when the boundary communicates a
control or state. The `.subtle` accent border variant is decorative; use it for visual decoration
that does not need to identify a boundary or state, not for a required control boundary. A required
boundary must remain distinguishable without relying on a low-contrast decorative border.

## Verify themes and states

Check the tag or label in its actual component, theme, and interaction states. Confirm that its hue
still means only the category, its text remains legible, and any required boundary stays visible.

## The palette

<ColorPaletteRamps />

## Picking colors for dark mode

Neutral colors are set up in a way that makes it easy to convert a light theme to a dark theme. If
you use our [design tokens](https://atlassian.design/tokens/design-tokens) to apply colors, this
mapping is done for you.

There is a light neutral ramp and an equivalent dark neutral ramp. Neutral100 in the light neutral
ramp should equate to DarkNeutral100 in the dark neutral ramp.

<img
	src={paletteNeutralMapping}
	alt="How light neutral ramp maps to dark neutral ramp in light and dark mode respectively."
/>

Saturated colors are also easy to convert from a light theme to a dark theme using symmetry. We
currently have 10 swatches set up in each palette. If the swatches are divided in half, each half
becomes a mirror.

<img
	src={paletteSaturatedMapping}
	alt="How colors in the blue ramp maps across light and dark mode."
/>

For instance, if a button color is 700 in light theme, it will be 400 in dark theme. If a section
message background is 100 in light theme, it will be 1000 in dark theme. Design tokens will handle
these conversions for you.

<img
	src={paletteJiraBoard}
	alt="Screenshot of a Confluence page, which is shown half in light mode and half in dark mode. The button background uses B700 in light mode and B400 in dark mode. The section message background uses B100 in light mode and B1000 in dark mode."
/>

Using symmetry is a great place to start and will cover most use cases. However, it may not work for
all use cases and color mapping can be adjusted accordingly.

